.TH NC 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
nc \- send and receive UDP datagrams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nc \-u
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.I address port
.br
.B nc \-u \-l
.RB [ \-p ]
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nc
(netcat) sends what it reads from standard input over the network, as
UDP datagrams through the computer's network cards, and writes the data of
the datagrams that come to it to standard output.
.PP
In the first form it sends each read (a line, from a terminal) as a
datagram to
.I port
at
.IR address :
a network card's address (its id, as
.B ifconfig
shows it), or
.B broadcast
for every card the computer's cards reach (a wired card's cables, a WLAN
card's range). What comes back to its port is written out. It ends when
standard input ends.
.PP
With
.B \-l
it listens on
.IR port :
it writes each datagram that comes, and sends what it reads to whoever
sent the last one, until it's stopped (^C).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
UDP. It's mcos's only transport, so it's needed, as for UDP on Linux.
.TP
.B \-l
Listens on
.I port
for datagrams.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
The port it sends from (or listens on, with
.BR \-l ).
Without it, a free one is given (32768 to 60999).
.TP
.B \-b
Taken, and needed for nothing: mcos sends to
.B broadcast
without SO_BROADCAST set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when standard input ends; 1 for a bad option or port, or when a datagram
can't be sent (no network card: "Network is unreachable"; no card took
it: "No route to host").
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
nc \-ul 7 > /tmp/heard
echo hello | nc \-u 1b9c0e6a\-6f0c\-4e0f\-9a43\-7d2f3c1a5e88 7
echo all | nc \-ub broadcast 7
.fi
.SH NOTES
mcos has UDP and no IP: an address is a network card's, and the network is
what the cards reach, with nothing routed between networks (a wired card
and a WLAN card are two networks). A datagram is lost when no program has
its port, or its queue is full, as UDP's are; one sent to a single card is
acknowledged by that card (Wi\-Fi does at its link layer), so a card that
isn't there is an error, as an unanswered ARP is on Linux. A datagram to
the computer's own card, or a broadcast, reaches its own programs too.
.PP
GNU and OpenBSD netcat stay after standard input ends, until
.B \-q
or
.B \-w
says; this one ends at once, as with
.BR "\-q 0" .
Without
.BR \-u ,
netcat would use TCP, which mcos doesn't have.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifconfig (1),
.BR ethtool (1)
